
{"id":"18faab4f-ff47-49fc-9aff-e03e385d2fa6","object":"chat.completion","created":1784962098,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) according to the task. The task is SE-PE-01: grammar-guided node reduction. The output schema requires \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\". The description mentions algorithm: Perses syntax-guided reduction. The input case has nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols. Objective: reduce the tree by removing nodes while preserving required symbols and interestingness oracle (implied by grammar validity?). The algorithm: Consider deeper nodes before shallower, never remove root, required symbol, or node with retained children. Tentatively remove only if all required symbols remain represented. Use node ID as tie break at equal depth. Return sorted retained IDs and actual removal order.\n\nWe need to parse the case, build tree structure (parent-child relationships). Then perform reduction. The algorithm: iterate nodes sorted by depth descending, then by id ascending for tie? Actually \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we process nodes in order of decreasing depth, and within same depth, order by node id? \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we can sort nodes by (-depth, id). For each node in that order, if removable is True, and it's not the root, and its symbol is not in required_symbols? But also need to check if any child that is retained? The description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node has children that are retained (i.e., not removed yet), we cannot remove it. Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So before removing, we need to check that after removal, all required symbols still exist in the tree (presumably among retained nodes). The required symbols must be present in some node that is retained. Since we are not actually removing permanently until we check? The algorithm likely simulates removal in order, and if removal would cause missing required symbol, skip. Also, removing a node also removes its subtree (descendants). Because Perses reduction removes a node and all its descendants. So when we consider removing a node, we need to ensure that all required symbols that were present in that subtree are also present elsewhere? Actually, required symbols are global: the tree must contain at least one node with that symbol. So if a required symbol only appears in the subtree of the node being removed, then removing that node would lose that required symbol, so we cannot remove it. Also, if the node itself has a required symbol, we cannot remove it because it's a required symbol? Actually the rule says \"never remove ... a required symbol\". So if the node's symbol is in required_symbols, we cannot remove it. So we check that. Also, if any descendant has a required symbol, and that symbol doesn't appear elsewhere, then removal would violate the \"all required symbols remain represented\". So we need to compute for each node the set of symbols present in its subtree (including itself). Then before removing, check that all required symbols are not solely dependent on this node's subtree.\n\nAlternatively, we can simulate removal: remove node and its subtree from the set of retained nodes, then check if all required symbols are still present in the remaining retained nodes. If yes, removal is valid, and we update the retained set. But we need to process nodes in order, and once we remove a node, its descendants are gone, so later nodes that are descendants are no longer considered. So we need to maintain a set of retained node IDs. Initially all nodes are retained? But some nodes are removable; we consider removal. The algorithm: start with all nodes retained. For each node in order (descending depth, then id), if the node is still retained (not removed previously) and removable and not root and symbol not in required_symbols, then tentatively remove it (i.e., remove its entire subtree from the retained set). Check if all required symbols still present. If yes, finalize removal (add node ID to removed list). If no, revert (restore the subtree). But careful: since we process deeper nodes first, when we remove a node, its children (which are deeper) have already been considered for removal? Actually deeper nodes are processed first, so if a child was removed earlier, the parent node might have no retained children. That's fine. But if we remove a parent later, it would remove any retained descendants that were not yet considered? But we process deeper first, so by the time we consider a parent, all its descendants have already been processed. So if we decide to remove the parent, its descendants are already either removed (if they were in the removed set) or retained (if they were not removed). But the parent removal will also remove those retained descendants, which may contain required symbols. So we need to check the effect of removing the parent on the entire subtree. Since we process deeper first, we might have already decided to keep some descendants because they were not removable or we chose not to remove them. So when considering parent, we need to consider removing the entire subtree as a block. This is consistent with Perses: you can remove a subtree if it is grammar-valid (i.e., the resulting tree after deletion is still valid). The \"grammar-valid\" is not explicitly defined here, but the task says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" and mentions Perses. The constraints given: never remove root, required symbol, node with retained children. But \"node with retained children\" means if a node has children that are retained (i.e., not removed), you cannot remove it because that would orphan those children? Actually in tree reduction, if you remove a node, you remove its entire subtree, including retained children. So the rule \"never remove a node with retained children\" likely means you can only remove a node if all its children have already been removed (or are also being removed). Since we process deeper first, when we consider a node, its children have already been processed and may have been removed. So if there is any child that is still retained, we cannot remove the parent because that would remove a retained child. So indeed we need to check if any child of the node is still retained. That's consistent with the rule.\n\nSo algorithm: \n- Build tree: map from node id to node dict, and children list for each node.\n- Determine initial retained set: all node IDs.\n- Determine order: sort nodes by (-depth, id). For each node in that order:\n   - If node not in retained: skip (already removed as part of ancestor removal).\n   - Check removable flag (true), not root, symbol not in required_symbols.\n   - Check if any child (direct child) is in retained set. If yes, skip (cannot remove because of retained children).\n   - Tentatively remove: compute set of nodes to remove = {node} union all descendants (by traversing subtree). We can precompute subtree IDs for each node, or compute on the fly.\n   - Remove these from retained set temporarily, and check if all required_symbols are still present in remaining retained nodes. (Required symbols: for each symbol in required_symbols, there exists at least one retained node with that symbol.)\n   - If yes, finalize removal: add node to removed list. Update retained set to the new set (permanent).\n   - If no, revert: restore the removed nodes to retained set.\n\n- After processing all nodes, the retained set contains nodes that were not removed. Then sort retained IDs? The output expects \"retained_node_ids\": sorted list of IDs? The task says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" The removal order is the order in which we removed nodes (the order we processed them and decided to remove). That order should be the sequence of node IDs in the order they were removed. For retained_node_ids, we need to sort them? Probably alphabetical/numerical? It says \"Return sorted retained IDs\". So we need to sort the retained node IDs. Typically lexicographically. We'll sort them as strings.\n\nAlso, we need to include node IDs as strings. The input nodes have \"id\" as string.\n\nNow, we need to consider if there are any other constraints: \"grammar_valid\" – we didn't use any grammar. Possibly the rule about required symbols is the only constraint. Also \"interestingness_preserved\" is equivalent to required symbols preserved. The task says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" So check that case has nodes, required_symbols, etc. Raise ValueError if infeasible: e.g., if required_symbols contain a symbol not present in any node? But case should be valid. Or if root is not present? Probably assume valid.\n\nWe must not mutate case.\n\nNow implement solve(case) returning dict with removed_node_ids (list in removal order) and retained_node_ids (sorted list). Use only Python standard library.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Check that case has 'nodes' and 'required_symbols'. If not, raise ValueError.\n2. Build dict node_map: id -> node dict.\n3. Build adjacency: children list per node. For each node, if parent is not None, add node id to parent's children list. Root has parent null.\n4. Compute subtree IDs for each node using DFS (to avoid recomputation). We'll need to quickly get all descendant IDs. We can precompute a set of descendant IDs for each node (including itself? Usually subtree includes node itself). We'll compute descendants (excluding node) separately for removal set.\n5. Initialize retained_set = set(all node ids).\n6. removed_order = [].\n7. Sort nodes list by (-depth, id). For each node in that order:\n   - if node id not in retained_set: continue.\n   - node = node_map[id].\n   - if not node['removable']: continue.\n   - if node['parent'] is None: continue (root not removable).\n   - if node['symbol'] in required_symbols: continue.\n   - # Check if any direct child is retained\n   - has_retained_child = any(child_id in retained_set for child_id in children.get(id, []))\n   - if has_retained_child: continue.\n   - # Tentative removal: compute set to remove: {id} + all descendants that are still in retained_set (since some may have been removed already)\n   - removal_candidates = {id}\n   - # Use stack to collect descendants that are currently retained\n   - stack = list(children.get(id, []))\n   - while stack:\n        child = stack.pop()\n        if child in retained_set:\n            removal_candidates.add(child)\n            stack.extend(children.get(child, []))\n   - # Compute new retained set if we remove these\n   - new_retained = retained_set - removal_candidates\n   - # Check required symbols: for each sym in required_symbols, check if any node in new_retained has that symbol.\n   - missing = False\n   - for sym in required_symbols:\n        if not any(node_map[nid]['symbol'] == sym for nid in new_retained):\n            missing = True\n            break\n   - if not missing:\n        # finalize removal\n        removed_order.append(id)\n        retained_set = new_retained\n   # else: do nothing, node remains retained\n\n8. After loop, retained_node_ids = sorted(retained_set) (as strings).\n9. removed_node_ids = removed_order (list in removal order).\n\nBut note: the removal order should be the order in which we actually removed nodes. Since we process in depth order and only remove if conditions hold, the removed_order list will be in that order. That's fine.\n\nEdge cases: If there are multiple nodes with same depth and same id? IDs are unique. Tie-breaking by node ID ensures deterministic order.\n\nOne nuance: The rule \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" We already check root and required symbol. For \"node with retained children\", we check direct children retained. But what about grandchildren? The removal of parent would also remove grandchildren, but if a grandchild is retained, it's because we decided to keep it. But we only check direct children. However, if a direct child is not retained (already removed), but a grandchild is retained, that grandchild is still a descendant. Can we remove the parent? The parent removal would remove that grandchild, which is retained. So we need to check if any descendant (not just direct child) is retained. Because if a grandchild is retained, it means we considered it earlier and decided not to remove it. So removing parent would remove that retained grandchild, which violates the idea that we should not remove a node that has retained descendants. But the rule says \"never remove a node with retained children.\" It might be interpreted as direct children only. However, in Perses, you can only remove a node if all its children have been removed (or are removable). Typically, you process bottom-up, so by the time you consider a parent, all its children have been considered and either removed or kept. If any child is kept, you cannot remove the parent because that would remove the kept child. But if a child was removed but a grandchild is kept? That seems impossible: if a child was removed, its entire subtree was removed, so no grandchild can be retained. So only direct children matter. Because we process bottom-up, if a node is removed, its entire subtree is gone. So if a child was removed, no descendant remains. Thus checking only direct children is sufficient. However, if we consider a node whose child was removed, but that child had a retained descendant? That can't happen because removal of child removes all descendants. So indeed, only direct children need to be checked.\n\nSo algorithm stands.\n\nNow, we need to produce the Python source as a JSON string. We'll write a function `solve(case)` that does the above and returns the dict.\n\nAlso, include error handling: raise ValueError if case is malformed.\n\nNow, output JSON must have key \"implementation\" with the source code string. We'll need to escape newlines, quotes, backslashes. Easier to write the source as a triple-quoted string and then use json.dumps to encode. But we must output the entire response as JSON with key \"implementation\". Use python to generate it? Since we are in text, we can manually write the JSON with escaped source. We'll write a Python script to generate the JSON, but given constraints, we need to output directly. We'll construct the source code string carefully.\n\nI'll write the solve function as:\n\n```\ndef solve(case):\n    # extract nodes and required_symbols\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\n    required_symbols = case.get('required_symbols')\n    if not nodes or not required_symbols:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing 'nodes' or 'required_symbols'\")\n    node_map = {}\n    children = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        node_map[nid] = n\n        children.setdefault(nid, [])\n    # build parent-child relationships\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n.get('parent')\n        if pid is not None:\n            if pid not in children:\n                children[pid] = []\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\n    # precompute subtree descendant sets (excluding self) for quick removal set\n    # actually we can compute on the fly using stack\n    # But we need to know if a node has any retained children? We'll check direct children from children dict.\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    removed_order = []\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        if not n['removable']:\n            continue\n        if n['parent'] is None:\n            continue\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # check if any direct child is retained\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, [])):\n            continue\n        # compute removal set: node and all descendants that are currently retained\n        to_remove = {nid}\n        stack = list(children.get(nid, []))\n        while stack:\n            cid = stack.pop()\n            if cid in retained:\n                to_remove.add(cid)\n                stack.extend(children.get(cid, []))\n        # simulate removal\n        candidate_retained = retained - to_remove\n        # check required symbols\n        missing = any(\n            not any(node_map[nid2]['symbol'] == sym for nid2 in candidate_retained)\n            for sym in required_symbols\n        )\n        if not missing:\n            removed_order.append(nid)\n            retained = candidate_retained\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted\n    }\n```\n\nBut need to ensure we handle case where children dict might not have entries for some nodes (leaf nodes have empty list). Already setdefault.\n\nPotential optimization: precompute mapping from symbol to list of node IDs. But fine.\n\nNow, we need to produce the output JSON. The entire response must be exactly one valid JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is a JSON-escaped string of the Python source.\n\nLet's construct that. We'll write the source code with proper indentation, then escape it. Since we can't use external tools, we'll do it manually by writing the JSON literal. We'll need to escape quotes, backslashes, newlines as \\n.\n\nTo avoid mistakes, we'll write the source as a multiline string in the response, but that would cause JSON to be invalid. We must escape newlines as \\n and double quotes as \\\". We'll do that.\n\nAlternatively, we can output the Python source in a single line with semicolons? But readability not important. However, to be safe, we'll write it as a single line with \\n for newlines.\n\nI'll write the implementation as:\n\n```python\nimplementation = \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\\n    required_symbols = case.get('required_symbols')\\n    if not nodes or not required_symbols:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing 'nodes' or 'required_symbols'\\\")\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = n\\n        children.setdefault(nid, [])\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n.get('parent')\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            if pid not in children:\\n                children[pid] = []\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if n['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        to_remove = {nid}\\n        stack = list(children.get(nid, []))\\n        while stack:\\n            cid = stack.pop()\\n            if cid in retained:\\n                to_remove.add(cid)\\n                stack.extend(children.get(cid, []))\\n        candidate_retained = retained - to_remove\\n        missing = any(\\n            not any(node_map[nid2]['symbol'] == sym for nid2 in candidate_retained)\\n            for sym in required_symbols\\n        )\\n        if not missing:\\n            removed_order.append(nid)\\n            retained = candidate_retained\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted\\n    }\"\n```\n\nThen the JSON is {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\\n    ...\"}\n\nWe need to be careful with escaping double quotes inside strings. In the source, we have e.g., case.get('nodes') uses single quotes, fine. But we have double quotes in the return statement and in error string. Those need to be escaped as \\\" in the JSON string value. Also backslashes in the source? Only newlines are \\n, but we also have a backslash in the lambda? Actually lambda uses colon, no backslash. But we have escaped quotes in the source: we wrote \\\" as literal in the source? In the above Python code snippet, we used double quotes for strings like \"Missing 'nodes' or 'required_symbols'\" and for dictionary keys in return. To embed that in a JSON string, we need to escape the double quotes. So we need to write \\\\\" for each double quote. Also newlines as \\\\n. So we will produce a string where each double quote that appears in the source is replaced with \\\\\", and each newline is replaced with \\\\n. Also any backslash in source (like if we had \\\\) would need to be \\\\\\\\, but there are none.\n\nWe'll construct the final JSON manually with proper escaping.\n\nLet me write the source as a single line with \\n but double quotes escaped. I'll write it out carefully.\n\nI'll replace a few quotes: In error string, we have double quotes around \"Missing 'nodes' or 'required_symbols'\", so in JSON we need \\\"Missing 'nodes' or 'required_symbols'\\\". Similarly, in return dict, keys have double quotes: \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\". So we need to escape those.\n\nAlso the lambda comparison uses double quotes? No, it uses n['depth'] etc. Single quotes fine.\n\nSo final implementation string:\n\n\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\\n    required_symbols = case.get('required_symbols')\\n    if not nodes or not required_symbols:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing 'nodes' or 'required_symbols'\\\")\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = n\\n        children.setdefault(nid, [])\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n.get('parent')\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            if pid not in children:\\n                children[pid] = []\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if n['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        to_remove = {nid}\\n        stack = list(children.get(nid, []))\\n        while stack:\\n            cid = stack.pop()\\n            if cid in retained:\\n                to_remove.add(cid)\\n                stack.extend(children.get(cid, []))\\n        candidate_retained = retained - to_remove\\n        missing = any(\\n            not any(node_map[nid2]['symbol'] == sym for nid2 in candidate_retained)\\n            for sym in required_symbols\\n        )\\n        if not missing:\\n            removed_order.append(nid)\\n            retained = candidate_retained\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted\\n    }\"\n\nNow embed in JSON: {\"implementation\":\"...the escaped string...\"}\n\nDouble-check escaping: In the source above, there are double quotes in the raise ValueError line: ValueError(\\\"Missing 'nodes' or 'required_symbols'\\\") -> that includes two double quotes around the error message. In the JSON string value, each of those double quotes must be escaped as \\\". So we need to replace \" with \\\" in the entire source string. Also the return dict has double quotes around keys. So we do that.\n\nWe also need to escape backslash if any. The source contains backslash in the lambda? No. It contains backslash in the line endings as \\n, but those are literal newline characters we are replacing with \\n (backslash-n) in the JSON string. So we need to actually write \\n as two characters: backslash and n. That's fine.\n\nSo we will construct the final JSON output:\n\n{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\\n    required_symbols = case.get('required_symbols')\\n    if not nodes or not required_symbols:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing 'nodes' or 'required_symbols'\\\")\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = n\\n        children.setdefault(nid, [])\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n.get('parent')\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            if pid not in children:\\n                children[pid] = []\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if n['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        to_remove = {nid}\\n        stack = list(children.get(nid, []))\\n        while stack:\\n            cid = stack.pop()\\n            if cid in retained:\\n                to_remove.add(cid)\\n                stack.extend(children.get(cid, []))\\n        candidate_retained = retained - to_remove\\n        missing = any(\\n            not any(node_map[nid2]['symbol'] == sym for nid2 in candidate_retained)\\n            for sym in required_symbols\\n        )\\n        if not missing:\\n            removed_order.append(nid)\\n            retained = candidate_retained\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted\\n    }\"}\n\nBut we need to escape double quotes inside the string. Let's do that step by step. I'll replace all occurrences of \" in the source with \\\", except the outer JSON structure. The source has several double quotes: \n- raise ValueError(\"...\") -> two double quotes\n- return { \"removed_node_ids\": ..., \"retained_node_ids\": ... } -> four double quotes (two keys, two values? Actually the values are variables, so just keys: \"removed_node_ids\": and \"retained_node_ids\": so four quotes? No: each key has a left quote and right quote, so two per key, total four. But also the string inside error has quotes, so total 6 double quotes. We need to escape each.\n\nLet me write the source with escaped double quotes manually:\n\n\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\\n    required_symbols = case.get('required_symbols')\\n    if not nodes or not required_symbols:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing 'nodes' or 'required_symbols'\\\")\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = n\\n        children.setdefault(nid, [])\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n.get('parent')\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            if pid not in children:\\n                children[pid] = []\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']: